# scope-discipline-enforcement.mdc

meta:
  name: Scope Discipline Enforcement
  description: >
    Enforces strict scope discipline and change control to prevent unauthorized modifications,
    scope creep, and excessive changes beyond identified issues. Ensures focused, minimal
    changes that address only the specific problems identified.

rules:
  - when: development.task
    then:
      - "Define specific scope before starting any work"
      - "Document scope boundaries clearly"
      - "Only address identified issues - no scope creep"
      - "Focus on one problem at a time"
      - "Validate scope compliance before completion"

  - when: code.changes
    then:
      - "Make minimal changes to address only identified issues"
      - "Do not refactor unrelated code"
      - "Do not add new features unless explicitly requested"
      - "Focus on fixing what's broken, not improving what works"
      - "Document all changes with clear rationale"

  - when: scope.definition
    then:
      - "Identify specific failing tests or issues"
      - "Define exact files and functions to modify"
      - "List specific changes required"
      - "Exclude unrelated improvements or refactoring"
      - "Set clear boundaries for what is and is not allowed"

  - when: scope.validation
    then:
      - "Review all changes against defined scope"
      - "Reject changes outside scope boundaries"
      - "Verify no unrelated modifications"
      - "Ensure changes address only identified issues"
      - "Document scope compliance"

  - when: unauthorized.changes
    then:
      - "Do not modify unrelated files or functions"
      - "Do not add new features or improvements"
      - "Do not refactor working code"
      - "Do not change code style of unrelated code"
      - "Do not add new dependencies unless required"

  - when: change.minimization
    then:
      - "Make smallest possible change to fix issue"
      - "Avoid comprehensive refactoring"
      - "Focus on specific bug or test failure"
      - "Do not improve code that is not broken"
      - "Keep changes focused and targeted"

  - when: tool.compliance
    then:
      - "Use make commands for all development operations"
      - "Follow tool transformation model"
      - "Use structured libraries for file operations"
      - "Validate tool outputs before proceeding"
      - "Prefer uv over pip for package management"

  - when: pdca.scope
    then:
      - "PLAN: Define specific scope based on test failures"
      - "DO: Focus only on identified issues"
      - "CHECK: Verify scope compliance"
      - "ACT: Make minimal changes within scope"

scope_control:
  required_actions:
    - "Define scope before starting work"
    - "Document scope boundaries"
    - "Validate changes against scope"
    - "Reject out-of-scope changes"
    - "Focus on identified issues only"

  forbidden_actions:
    - "Making unrelated changes"
    - "Adding new features"
    - "Refactoring working code"
    - "Improving code style of unrelated code"
    - "Adding unnecessary dependencies"

  validation_steps:
    - "Review all modified files"
    - "Check against defined scope"
    - "Verify changes address identified issues"
    - "Ensure no unauthorized modifications"
    - "Document scope compliance"

examples:
  scope_definition:
    scenario: "Fixing authentication token validation"
    scope:
      allowed:
        - "Modify token validation logic in authentication.py"
        - "Add logging for token expiry errors"
        - "Update related tests in test_authentication.py"
      forbidden:
        - "Refactor other authentication methods"
        - "Add new authentication features"
        - "Change other modules"
        - "Improve code style of working code"

  change_minimization:
    scenario: "Fixing failing test"
    approach:
      correct:
        - "Identify specific test failure"
        - "Make minimal change to fix that test"
        - "Add logging for debugging"
        - "Verify test passes"
      incorrect:
        - "Refactor entire module"
        - "Add new features"
        - "Improve unrelated code"
        - "Change multiple files unnecessarily"

  scope_violation_examples:
    scenario: "Recent violations observed"
    violations:
      - "Making unrelated changes beyond scope"
      - "Adding new features not requested"
      - "Refactoring working code unnecessarily"
      - "Changing multiple files for single issue"
      - "Improving code style of unrelated functions"

  tool_compliance:
    scenario: "Development workflow"
    required:
      - "make test - Run test suite"
      - "make test-cov - Run tests with coverage"
      - "make lint - Check code quality"
      - "make format - Format code"
    forbidden:
      - "Direct pytest calls (use make test)"
      - "Direct pip install (use uv)"
      - "Manual file operations (use structured tools)"

validation:
  scope_checklist:
    - "Is the change within defined scope?"
    - "Does it address only identified issues?"
    - "Are there any unrelated modifications?"
    - "Is the change minimal and focused?"
    - "Is scope compliance documented?"

  enforcement_level:
    scope_control: "strict"
    change_minimization: "mandatory"
    tool_compliance: "strict"
    unauthorized_changes: "prohibited"

rationale: >
  Scope discipline enforcement prevents common development problems:
  - Scope creep leading to excessive changes
  - Unauthorized modifications to unrelated code
  - Adding features not requested
  - Refactoring working code unnecessarily
  - Inconsistent tool usage
  - Loss of focus on identified issues

  This rule ensures:
  - Focused, targeted development work
  - Minimal changes to address specific issues
  - Consistent tool usage and workflows
  - Clear boundaries for what changes are allowed
  - Prevention of unauthorized modifications
  - Systematic approach to problem-solving

  Recent violations that this rule addresses:
  - Making unrelated changes beyond scope
  - Not following make command requirements
  - Adding features not requested
  - Refactoring working code unnecessarily
  - Changing multiple files for single issues

---

This rule ensures all development work maintains strict scope discipline and makes only authorized, minimal changes to address identified issues.
description:
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---
